1. Cap mapping — a cap means "never above ratio R". Replace _cap_khz use in caps with a cap-specific function in controls/caps.py:
   choose the LARGEST 100 MHz bin N (8 <= N <= cpuinfo_max/100000) whose predicted achieved ratio <= R, using the MEASURED model of
   docs/knowledge/power-profile-capping.md §6 (cite it): E (cpuinfo max 4900000): p = ceil(1.377*N), ratio = floor(p/1.4);
   P: p = ceil(1.595*N), ratio = floor(p/1.6). Use exact integer arithmetic (1377*N, 1595*N with ceil-division; floor(p*10/14), floor(p*10/16)).
   The model is only known for E with cpuinfo_max_freq 4900000 and P with 5500000/5700000; any other cpuinfo -> N = R (never overshoots, since a<step).
   Plan rows report the ratio the cap will actually give and flag "R unreachable by a cpufreq cap, capped at R-1" (or lower) when it differs.
   Tests: E 46 -> 4700000 (46x), E 40 -> 4000000, E 24 -> 2400000 (24x, NOT 2500000), E 23 -> 2300000 or the bin giving 22x and flagged unreachable,
   E 28 -> 2900000, E 48 -> 4900000, P 42 -> flagged (41x), P 55 -> 5500000. Do not change ops_stress._cap_khz (stress/measure keep their retry logic).
